## Method and reasoning
For each length retain the smallest possible last value, which offers the most room for future values. These tails increase strictly. Replace the first tail at least x, or append x if none exists. Equal values replace a tail without extending the length.

## Walk through the sample
The tails progress [4], [1], [1], [1,3], [1,2], [1,2,5]. The second 1 replaces the existing tail. Extending on equality would incorrectly report length 2 for [4,1,1].

## Complexity
Time O(n log n), space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> tails;
    while (n--) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        auto at = lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);
        if (at == tails.end())
            tails.push_back(x);
        else
            *at = x;
        cout << tails.size() << '\n';
    }
}
